@flexiarg apparatus/new-failure-class-is-a-design-defect
@title A New Failure Class Is A Design Defect, Not A New Species
@keywords failure-taxonomy, park-classes, census, catch-layers, capitulation
@audience apparatus builders, campaign stewards, futon developers
@tone technical
@style pattern
@status promoted (field trial 2026-09-08, APM repair program S1-S10; evidence: futon3c/holes/technotes/TN-apm-principle-scoring-ledger.md @ b0c36489; Joe's ruling 2026-09-08)
@references [apparatus/evidence-to-disposition-once apparatus/replayable-not-precious]
@see-also apparatus/loudness-is-conserved
@violation-signature The class census grew this wave; a handler, rescue route, or park class was added for a failure shape rather than the shape being made impossible; the same detector or classifier has been widened once per incident.
! conclusion: Treat every NEW failure class as a defect in the design that admitted it — eliminate it one layer down; the census of classes is closed, shrinking, and its growth rate is the reported metric.

  + context: A running apparatus surfaces failures in classes (park reasons, error codes, stall causes), and each new class arrives with an obvious local response: catch it, name it, handle it.

  + IF:
    A failure appears that fits no existing class.

  + HOWEVER:
    Adding the class is always cheaper today than removing its cause, and each addition is locally reasonable — so the taxonomy grows monotonically, the handling machinery grows with it, and "reduce the failure rate" quietly becomes "improve the response".

  + THEN:
    Reverse the default. A new class opens a design question — what one layer down would make this shape impossible? — and the catch layer is the fallback only when that question has a recorded negative answer. Report the census and its growth rate wherever the apparatus reports health (the wm steward contract already requires growth rate alongside size — keep the two artifacts cross-cited).

  + BECAUSE:
    Each catch layer is capitulation to a failure that something one layer down should have made impossible, and the sum of capitulations is a system that survives everything and completes nothing.

    + evidence: APM: 111 of 126 parks were apparatus, in ~40 mostly-singleton classes; the singletons trace to per-consumer evidence joins (see apparatus/evidence-to-disposition-once) — one seam, forty species. WM: three stall-key widenings vs one whole-row hash.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    - Snapshot your census now; at the next wave boundary, diff it. Growth names the seams to fix.
    - For each singleton class, ask which existing class it would merge into if the one-layer-down fix existed.
